// Lesson 1.3 — Your First Script: Variables & Update
// First-introduction lesson: FULL scaffold (walkthrough level 1).
// The walkthrough gives PlayerLogger.cs complete and explains every line,
// because this is the first script these students have ever written.
//
// This lesson also owns the concept that everything downstream depends on:
// SCOPE AND LIFETIME — where you declare a variable decides whether it can
// remember anything between frames. 1.4's movement bug (declaring the input
// variable inside Update) is a symptom of this concept not landing here.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the first script these students
// ever write. Level 1, so every part is given with what it does AND where to
// read about it — nobody has split a problem before, so the first split is
// demonstrated rather than asked for. The four parts are the walkthrough's
// four steps in order: a file Unity will run, the state that has to be
// remembered, the once-only setup, and the every-frame gate. The split is
// doing real teaching, not decoration: part two is the scope rule the whole
// lesson is built to land, and part four is where the interval lives.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'Get a script of my own printing to the Console — "how do I print text to the console?" — ' +
    'and keep printing once a second instead of sixty times, so the Console is still readable a ' +
    'minute later.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Make the file something Unity will run',
      does: 'One class in the file, the class name matching the filename exactly, inheriting `MonoBehaviour` so Unity can attach it to a GameObject as a component. A file that gets this wrong never runs at all.',
      doc: 'the `class Boss : MonoBehaviour` row in the quick reference, and the component list you read in 1.2.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Put the two values that have to be remembered in the class body',
      does: 'A `reportDelay` tuning knob and a `nextReportTime` deadline, both declared outside every method. This is the placement decision the lesson turns on: here they are created once and survive the frame, while the same lines inside `Update()` would be rebuilt and thrown away sixty times a second.',
      doc: 'the declare-vs-assign split in the One Rule section below, and the `[SerializeField] private float speed;` row in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Give the deadline its first value in Start()',
      does: '`Start()` runs once, before the first frame. It sets `nextReportTime` to the current game time plus the delay, and prints one line so you know the script attached and ran.',
      doc: 'the `void Start()` and `Time.time` rows in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Gate the print in Update() and re-arm the deadline',
      does: '`Update()` runs every frame, which is far too often to print. The `if` asks whether the deadline has passed; if it has, print, then push the deadline one second into the future so the gate closes again.',
      doc: 'the `void Update()`, `Debug.Log` and `Time.time` rows in the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Four parts, and only the last one does anything you can see. The first three exist because ' +
    'of one question — does anything next frame need to know about this? — asked three times: yes ' +
    'for the deadline, no for the message, and once for the class itself. Nearly every script you ' +
    'write this year splits the same way: a thing Unity will run, the state it has to remember, the ' +
    'setup, and the every-frame work.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test for a finished split: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one ' +
    'search? "Print to the Console" cannot be looked up. `Debug.Log` can, and so can `Time.time`.',
  turns: [
    'Four parts, and only the last one does anything you can see. Which of the four would you ' +
      'have written first if nobody had shown you the split — and what would have gone wrong?',
  ],
};

const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I print text to the console?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerLogger.cs',
  intro: 'By the end you will have a script that prints to the Console once per second — and you will be able to explain every line of it.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Start the file and name the class',
      lead: 'A C# file holds one class. The name of the class and the name of the file have to match exactly — Unity uses the filename to find your class.',
      lines: [
        { code: 'using UnityEngine;', what: 'Tells C# where to find everything Unity provides — `MonoBehaviour`, `Debug`, `Time`. Without it, none of those names exist.' },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: 'public class PlayerLogger : MonoBehaviour', what: 'Declares a class called `PlayerLogger`. The `: MonoBehaviour` part is what makes it a **component** — a script Unity can attach to a GameObject.', why: 'The name after `class` must match the filename exactly, capitals included. `PlayerLogger` in `PlayerLogger.cs` works; `playerLogger` in `PlayerLogger.cs` throws an error the moment you press Play.' },
        { code: '{', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Put the tuning knobs in the class body',
      lead: 'These two lines sit **inside the class braces but outside any method**. That placement is the whole point of this step — see "The One Rule" above.',
      lines: [
        { code: '    [SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;', what: 'How many seconds between reports. The `[SerializeField]` tag makes a private field editable in the Inspector; the `f` suffix tells C# this is a `float` (a decimal number) and not a `double`.', why: 'It lives in the class body so it is created **once**, when the Player is created, and keeps its value for the whole game. If you moved this line into `Update()`, it would be rebuilt 60 times a second and any change you made to it would be thrown away instantly.' },
        { code: '    private float nextReportTime;', what: 'When the next report is allowed to happen. `private` means no other script may touch it — this is PlayerLogger\'s own bookkeeping.', why: 'Notice it has **no value yet**. A field does not have to be given a value at the moment you declare it. `Start()` will give it one.' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Write Start() — the once-only setup',
      lead: '`Start()` is a lifecycle method. Unity calls it for you, exactly once, before the first frame.',
      lines: [
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Start()', what: 'The method Unity runs once when the object appears. `void` means it hands nothing back when it finishes.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;', what: 'Sets the deadline for the first report: the current game time, plus one second. `Time.time` is how many seconds have passed since you pressed Play.', why: 'This is an **assignment**, not a declaration. `nextReportTime` was already declared up in the class body — here we are only giving it a value. Compare this with the line in the next step that declares *and* assigns at once.' },
        { code: '        Debug.Log("PlayerLogger is alive.");', what: 'Prints one line to the Unity Console so you know the script attached and ran.' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Write Update() — the every-frame gate',
      lead: '`Update()` runs once per frame — roughly 60 times a second. That is far too often to print anything, so the `if` is what keeps the Console readable.',
      lines: [
        { code: '', what: '' },
        { code: '    void Update()', what: 'The method Unity runs every single frame, automatically.' },
        { code: '    {', what: '' },
        { code: '        if (Time.time >= nextReportTime)', what: 'The gate. "Has enough time passed that it is legal to report again?" The `>=` means *at or past*, and the `==` you might expect would be wrong — frames are not exact.' , why: 'Without this check, the `Debug.Log` below would fire ~60 times per second and bury your Console.' },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        { code: '            Debug.Log("Player has been running for " + Time.time + " seconds.");', what: 'Prints the message. The `+` glues the text and the number into one string.' },
        { code: '            nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;', what: 'Moves the deadline one second into the future, so the gate above closes again.' , why: 'Delete this line and the Console floods: the gate would still be open on the next frame, so it would print on every single frame from then on. This is the most common bug in the whole lesson.' },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
        { code: '    }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

public class PlayerLogger : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float reportDelay = 1.0f;

    private float nextReportTime;

    void Start()
    {
        nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;
        Debug.Log("PlayerLogger is alive.");
    }

    void Update()
    {
        if (Time.time >= nextReportTime)
        {
            Debug.Log("Player has been running for " + Time.time + " seconds.");
            nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;
        }
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'This is the finished file. Do not paste it in — compare it against what you built. If a line differs from yours, you should now be able to say exactly what yours would do differently.',
};

const body = `<h1>1.3 — Your First Script: Variables &amp; Update</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Today you write your first C#. This is the lesson where everything else in the year
    starts, so we are going to go slowly: you will build one small script, and every
    single line of it gets explained before you type the next one. Nobody is expected
    to guess anything today.
</p>

<div class="callout info">
    <strong>Reading this on your own?</strong> This lesson covers the ground your teacher
    demonstrated live in class. Work through it in order and you will end up in exactly
    the same place as everyone else. The script you build here (<code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>)
    is a throwaway diagnostic tool, not part of the game. Its job is to teach you how a
    script attaches to a GameObject, what <code>Start()</code> and <code>Update()</code>
    actually do, and how to make the Console tell you what your code is thinking —
    three things you will rely on for the rest of the year.
</div>

${renderAskBanner('how do I print text to the console?', 1)}

${renderDecompose('1.3', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read the Docs</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~12 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every script in Unity that attaches to a GameObject inherits from <code>MonoBehaviour</code>.
        That base class is what connects your code to the engine's game loop. These are the
        five pieces of it you need today — keep this table open while you build:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Engine API</th><th>Signature / Type</th><th>Description &amp; Behavior</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Start()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Start()</code></td>
            <td>Lifecycle hook. Called automatically by Unity exactly once, before the first frame. Used for setup and for giving your fields their starting values.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Update()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Update()</code></td>
            <td>Lifecycle hook. Called automatically every single frame. At 60 FPS this runs 60 times per second. Used for polling input, checking timers, and continuous logic.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Debug.Log()</code></td>
            <td><code>void Debug.Log(object message)</code></td>
            <td>Prints a value to the Unity Console window. Developer feedback — the only way to see what your code is doing while it runs.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Time.time</code></td>
            <td><code>float</code> (read-only)</td>
            <td>Total elapsed seconds since the game entered Play mode. Constantly increases while the game runs; resets when you stop.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>[SerializeField]</code></td>
            <td>Attribute: <code>[SerializeField] private float speed;</code></td>
            <td>Tells Unity to show a private field as an editable control in the Inspector, without exposing it to other scripts.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Lifecycle and Timing</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>If your computer runs the game at 120 FPS, how many times will <code>Update()</code> execute in a single second? What if it runs on a battery-saving laptop at 30 FPS?</li>
            <li>If you write <code>Debug.Log("hello")</code> directly inside <code>Update()</code> with no check around it, what happens to your Unity Console in 5 seconds?</li>
            <li>Why does software engineering encourage <code>[SerializeField] private</code> rather than making every field <code>public</code>? (Hint: who should be allowed to change a component's internal variables?)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The One Rule</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Before you write any code today, read this section twice.</strong> Almost every
        confusing bug you will hit in the next two weeks comes from one misunderstanding, and
        it is not about Unity at all — it is about <em>where</em> you declare a variable.
        Get this right and the rest of the unit is straightforward.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>The rule:</strong> a variable declared <em>inside a method</em> is born when the
        method starts and destroyed when the method ends. It cannot remember anything between
        calls. A variable declared <em>in the class body</em> — outside every method — belongs
        to the object: it is created once and lives as long as the object does.
    </p>

    <p>
        Why this bites so hard: <code>Update()</code> runs about 60 times every second. So a
        variable declared inside <code>Update()</code> is created 60 times a second, and each
        copy starts from scratch and is thrown away. Watch what that does to a counter:
    </p>

    <div class="concept-compare">
        <div class="cc-side bad">
            <span class="cc-tag">Declared inside the method — broken</span>
<pre><code>void Update()
{
    int framesSeen = 0;
    framesSeen = framesSeen + 1;
    Debug.Log(framesSeen);
}</code></pre>
            <p>
                Prints <code>1</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>1</code>, <code>1</code>… forever.
                The <code>0</code> is re-applied every frame, so the counter is
                <em>reborn at zero</em> before it ever gets to add anything. It has no memory.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cc-side good">
            <span class="cc-tag">Declared in the class body — works</span>
<pre><code>private int framesSeen = 0;

void Update()
{
    framesSeen = framesSeen + 1;
    Debug.Log(framesSeen);
}</code></pre>
            <p>
                Prints <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>3</code>, <code>4</code>… The
                field belongs to the object, so it survives from one frame to the next and
                actually accumulates.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Now the part that catches everyone.</strong> Look closely at this line:
    </p>

<pre><code>float steeringIntent = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");</code></pre>

    <p>
        That single line is doing <em>two separate jobs at once</em>. Reading it as one action
        is where people go wrong. Pull the two jobs apart:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Declaring</strong> — <code>float steeringIntent</code> — creates the variable and says it holds a decimal number.</li>
        <li><strong>Assigning</strong> — <code>= Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code> — gives it a value.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Writing them on one line is legal, and it is perfectly fine when you only need the
        value for the next line or two. But when you need a value to <em>still be there on the
        next frame</em> — the direction the player is holding, how much health is left, how many
        coins you have collected — you must split the two jobs and put them in different places:
    </p>

<pre><code>private float steeringIntent;              // DECLARE: in the class body, created once

void Update()
{
    steeringIntent = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");   // ASSIGN: in Update, refreshed each frame
}</code></pre>

    <p>
        Same two jobs, deliberately separated. The variable now exists on the object, so it
        survives between frames, and <code>Update()</code> simply gives it a fresh value 60
        times a second. <em>This split is the shape you will use for every piece of state in
        this game.</em> You will meet it again in <a href="1.4.html">1.4</a>, where getting it
        wrong is the difference between a character that moves and one that twitches.
    </p>

    <div class="callout warn">
        <strong>A rule of thumb you can carry.</strong> If you want a value to be <em>read and
        used right now</em>, declaring it inside the method is fine. If you want it to be
        <em>remembered</em>, it goes in the class body. Ask "does anything next frame need to
        know about this?" — the answer tells you where the line belongs.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Check the Rule Landed</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>A student wants to count how many coins the player has collected. Should <code>coinCount</code> be declared in the class body or inside <code>OnTriggerEnter</code>? Say why, in terms of what happens on the next frame.</li>
            <li>In the broken counter above, the code reads <code>framesSeen = framesSeen + 1;</code> and still prints 1. How would you explain that result to someone who has not read this page?</li>
            <li>Why is it <em>not</em> a bug to declare a variable inside <code>Update()</code> when you use it immediately on the very next line and then never again?</li>
            <li>Look back at the <code>[SerializeField] private float speed;</code> row in the table above. Now that you know the rule, why does that line <em>have</em> to live in the class body?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

