<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        You now have a working logger. This section is where you prove to yourself that
        you understand <em>why</em> it works — by breaking it on purpose, one piece at a time,
        and predicting what happens before you press Play.
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the Logger on Purpose</h3>
        <p>Predict the result first, then try it, then fix it:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Move the line <code>nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;</code> from the class body into the top of <code>Update()</code>, deleting the field. What does the Console do now? Connect this back to the counter example above.</li>
            <li>Rename the class to <code>playerLogger</code> (lowercase p) while leaving the file named <code>PlayerLogger.cs</code>. What error appears when you press Play?</li>
            <li>Attach the script to the Turret instead of the Player. Does it still run? What does that prove about the relationship between components and GameObjects?</li>
            <li>Comment out <code>nextReportTime = Time.time + reportDelay;</code> inside the <code>if</code>. What happens to the Console within 2 seconds, and why does the gate never close again?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>


    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Create a new C# script and attach it to a GameObject as a component</li>
            <li>Explain what runs when: <code>Start()</code> once vs <code>Update()</code> every frame</li>
            <li><strong>Explain, without looking, why a variable declared inside <code>Update()</code> cannot remember anything — and where it should go instead</strong></li>
            <li>Split a declaration from an assignment, and say why you sometimes must</li>
            <li>Write a <code>[SerializeField] private</code> field and explain how it differs from a public field</li>
            <li>State the rule that a C# class name must match its filename exactly</li>
            <li>Use <code>Time.time</code> to run something on an interval instead of every frame</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

